CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0183a7-83184018-0184-87170e31-00006277:Hinz\, Nic
 olle
CREATED:20260929T151345Z
DESCRIPTION:Species/strain diversity (i.e.\, identity determined by geneti
 cs) and physiology or conformation (i.e.\, functional/physical state dete
 rmined by "lived experience") have the potential to influence the inactiv
 ation or decay kinetics of microbial contaminants\, such as bacteria or D
 NA. Nonetheless\, most disinfection research utilizes reference strain or
 ganisms or model systems cultivated in the laboratory for experiments use
 d to assess disinfection kinetics\, with limited discussion of the enviro
 nmental relevance of the observed disinfection behavior. To illustrate th
 e importance of identity and lived experience on disinfection kinetics\, 
 two examples from research in the Silverman Laboratory will be presented.
  The first is an evaluation of divergent inactivation kinetics of wastewa
 ter-sourced E. coli and enterococci\, laboratory-cultured E. coli and thr
 ee species of Enterococcus with exposure to commonly used disinfectants (
 i.e.\, free chlorine\, monochloramine\, UVC\, and simulated sunlight). Th
 e second is an assessment of the effect of DNA plasmid conformation on ob
 served sunlight-mediated decay kinetics of the antibiotic resistance gene
  ampR. Implications for experimental design\, and the design and regulati
 on of disinfection processes and environmental monitoring strategies for 
 microbial contaminants will be addressed.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260929T151345Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260929T151345Z
LOCATION;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf6e-00000000:None
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CEE Seminar - Environmental Relevance in Disinfection Research? In
 fluence of Species\, Conformation\, and “Lived Experience” on Decay Kinet
 ics of Microbial Contaminants
UID:CAL-8a002cbd-9fb2e569-01a0-edba9eba-000026d5demobedework@mysite.edu
