CREATED:20260729T152204Z
DESCRIPTION:The Sperling Center for Jewish Studies welcomes Ari Joskowicz\
 , who will give a lecture entitled "Money and Memory: How Funding Shaped 
 the Documentation of Jewish History and the Holocaust."  Joskowicz is the
  Eugene Greener Jr. Chair in Jewish Studies and Professor of Jewish Studi
 es and History at Vanderbilt University\, where he also directs the Max K
 ade Center for European and German Studies. A historian of modern Jewish 
 and European history\, his scholarship explores the intersections of Jewi
 sh history\, minority politics\, memory\, and the Holocaust\, with partic
 ular attention to the relationships between Jewish and Romani communities
  in Europe.\n\nJoskowicz is the author of the award-winning books Rain of
  Ash: Roma\, Jews\, and the Holocaust (Princeton University Press\, 2023)
  and The Modernity of Others: Jewish Anti-Catholicism in Germany and Fran
 ce (Stanford University Press\, 2014). His research has been recognized w
 ith major honors\, including the Ernst Fraenkel Prize of the Wiener Libra
 ry and the George L. Mosse Prize from the American Historical Association
  for Rain of Ash.\n\nThrough his teaching and scholarship\, Joskowicz exa
 mines how histories of persecution\, displacement\, and memory shape our 
 understanding of modern Europe. His work draws on a wide range of archiva
 l sources and has contributed significantly to contemporary discussions o
 f Holocaust history\, minority experiences\, and the politics of remembra
 nce.
DURATION:PT1H30M
DTSTAMP:20260827T104709Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T170000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260827T104709Z
