CONTACT;ALTREP="https://wcsj.law.duke.edu/";X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0182b3-870a19
 1e-0187-5a5c98e9-00003e5b:Melton\, Jennifer
CREATED:20261009T185808Z
DESCRIPTION:Four leaders who have spent decades representing people condem
 ned to die and changing North Carolina's criminal legal system discuss wh
 at justice demands and what it takes to keep fighting for it.\n\nFeaturin
 g:\n\nHenderson Hill\nCo-Founder & Co-Director\, RedressNC\n\nShelagh Ken
 ney\nExecutive Director\, Center for Death Penalty Litigation\n\nKristin 
 Parks\nAttorney & Criminal Justice Reform Advocate\n\nGerda Stein\nCrimin
 al Justice Reform Advocate & Former Mitigation Specialist\n\nLaw School R
 oom 4047\n\nDinner provided\n\nWhat does it take to pursue justice within
  a system that can inflict profound and irreversible harm-and how do you 
 sustain that work over a lifetime?Join the Duke Justice Project and Wilso
 n Center for Science and Justice for a conversation with four longtime le
 aders in North Carolina criminal justice reform. Drawing on decades of ex
 perience in capital defense\, litigation\, policymaking\, advocacy\, miti
 gation\, and reentry\, panelists will explore how lawyers and advocates h
 ave challenged the death penalty and other inequities within the criminal
  legal system while working to affirm the dignity and humanity of the peo
 ple it affects.\n\nThe conversation will go beyond examining what is wron
 g with the criminal legal system to ask how change actually happens: How 
 do litigation\, legislation\, research\, storytelling\, and coalition-bui
 lding work together? How do advocates engage people who fundamentally dis
 agree with them? What do policymakers misunderstand about the people affe
 cted by the policies they create? And how can people committed to difficu
 lt\, emotionally demanding work sustain their sense of purpose without sa
 crificing their own wellbeing?\n\nDesigned for students interested in law
 \, public policy\, advocacy\, public service\, and social change\, the ev
 ening will begin with a moderated conversation followed by audience Q&A.\
 n\nCo-sponsored by the Duke Justice Project and the Wilson Center for Sci
 ence and Justice
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20261009T185808Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T180000
LAST-MODIFIED:20261009T185808Z
